  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1228 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nOppressive heat and humidity has returned to the Mid-South with\nmany locations already reporting heat index values approaching or\nexceeding 105 degrees. Temperatures will continue to warm into\nthe afternoon, so exercise caution if you must be outside for\nprolonged periods and drink plenty of water. Based on the latest\nguidance and trends, the Extreme Heat Warning was expanded\nslightly in portions of West TN. Outside of the heat today, all\neyes will turn north as a MCV will develop later today into\ntonight across the Ohio River Valley. CAM guidance has remained\nlargely spread with this event, but the general consensus does\nkeep the strongest storms to our north and east as the system\nsurges to the south. However, if the complex were to push into\nour area or develop further west, most guidance does show a\nfairly robust environment with well over 3,500 J/kg of SBCAPE and\nfairly healthy lapse rates. A limiting factor into the late\nevening to overnight hours will be CIN and developing surface\ninversion, which would again suggest that the MCV should be\ndecaying as it reaches our area. Nonetheless, damaging winds will\nbe the primary concern, with periods of heavy rainfall if the\ncomplex pushes through. Confidence remains very low, but there is\nenough uncertainty that it is worth keeping an eye on into the\nevening and overnight hours.\n\nAny lingering shower and thunderstorm activity will quickly move\nout by Monday morning, with temperatures surging and an even\nwarmer and humid airmass remaining in place. High temperatures\nare forecast to reach the mid to upper 90s on Monday and Tuesday,\nbut some locations may reach 100 degrees especially on Tuesday.\nFurther expansion of the Extreme Heat Warning may be needed, but\nadjustments will be made in later forecast packages if necessary.\n\nBy Wednesday, an upper-level trough will continue to develop\nacross the northeast U.S. with a backdoor frontal boundary easing\nthe oppressive heat and humidity across our area. As such, some\nheat products may still be needed across the Delta regions before\nthe boundary arrives, but most of the Mid-South will likely fall\nback to closer to summer-time normal values with highs in the low\n90s. This upper-level, northwesterly flow will allow for daily\nthunderstorm chances to return to the Mid-South and aid in\nkeeping highs in the low 90s to end the week.",
